plan_label_schema — implementation plan
=======================================

The current A/I/D/Q/T addresses are valuable, but their meaning and lifecycle
are partly conventional.  A strict evidence gate would otherwise encourage a
test to be attached to a dead assumption, treat an old design note like an
acceptance promise, or leave a resolved question looking open forever.

goal
----

Give every labelled plan record a typed meaning and explicit state before the
planning gate enforces evidence completeness.  Preserve stable qualified IDs
and make historical ambiguity visible rather than guessing it away.

label schema
------------

`A` — assumption or acceptance claim
  An observable behavior the implementation relies on or promises.  ACTIVE A
  records require registered verification.  MOOT or SUPERSEDED records retain
  their IDs and require a dated rationale or a resolving ID, but no fabricated
  test.

`I` — shared infrastructure assumption
  Declared and verified once by its owning plan.  Later plans reference the
  qualified ID and do not duplicate its evidence.  Its lifecycle states match
  A.

`D` — design, detail, or dependency record
  Every D is classified as DESIGN, DETAIL, or DEPENDENCY.  A dependency carries
  a resolvable target.  Observable behavior discovered in a historical D moves
  to A; a concrete verification requirement moves to T.  D does not acquire a
  test merely to improve coverage.

`Q` — design question or implementation choice
  Explicitly OPEN or RESOLVED.  A resolved Q has a date and resolution text or
  a target A/D/T/ADR.  An OPEN Q prevents its plan from becoming done.

`T` — concrete test requirement
  States the behavior to verify and names every required test layer.  Every
  layer is required and will need a distinct covering test case.

New ACTIVE A, ACTIVE I, and T records must name their layers explicitly.
`unstated` remains a legacy condition to inventory, not a valid new value.

migration
---------

1. Extend the v1 claim schema with `kind`, `state`, `statement`, `layers`, and
   kind-specific fields such as `target`, `resolution`, and `resolved_on`.
2. Inventory the current registry by label kind and state.  Emit a review file
   containing only semantic decisions: prose-marked MOOT/SUPERSEDED A records,
   all historical D records, Q records, and ACTIVE A/I/T records with inferred
   or unstated layers.
3. Human-review that bounded decision file.  Automated migration may move
   syntax but may not decide whether behavior is moot, observable, or required.
4. Migrate accepted decisions into v1 frontmatter while preserving plan name,
   label, statement, and qualified ID.
5. Generate a richer registry containing kind, state, layer set, and owner.
   Keep the existing text and Isabelle ID outputs for compatibility.
6. Make references resolve to the owning qualified ID.  Reject duplicate
   owners, dangling supersession/dependency/resolution targets, and cycles in
   supersession.

compatibility claims (canonical until v1 lands)
------------------------------------------------

A1 [tooling-unit]: Every ACTIVE A record represents behavior that must be
    verified before its owner plan is done.

A2 [tooling-unit]: A MOOT or SUPERSEDED A record keeps its stable ID and an
    auditable reason; it does not receive a false verification link.

I1 [tooling-unit]: An ACTIVE I record is verified by its owner exactly once and
    can be reused through qualified references without duplicate declarations.

D1. A D record is structurally checked, but test completeness is not inferred
    from the historically ambiguous D prefix.

D2. A behavioral historical D becomes A and a concrete verification
    requirement becomes T; the migration records the old qualified ID as an
    alias or superseded record so references do not silently break.

T1 [tooling-unit]: ACTIVE, MOOT, and SUPERSEDED A/I fixture records validate
    only with their required rationale or target fields.

T2 [tooling-unit]: A dependency D with an unknown target is rejected; DESIGN
    and DETAIL D records do not require test metadata.

T3 [tooling-unit]: An OPEN Q prevents done; a RESOLVED Q without a date and
    resolution is rejected.

T4 [tooling-unit]: A new ACTIVE A/I/T record with no explicit layer is rejected.

T5 [tooling-unit]: Downstream references to an I record resolve to one owner and
    duplicate declarations are rejected.

T6 [tooling-unit]: Migration fixtures preserve qualified IDs and reject an
    unreviewed semantic conversion between A, D, and T.

non-goals
---------

- Proving a test genuinely establishes an assumption; that remains review work.
- Requiring tests for pure design/detail records.
- Deleting historical IDs merely because their claims became moot.
